From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:35:32 +1100
Subject: soc: apple: sep: select the platform profile by SoC

On T8122, T6040, T6041, T6050, T8132 and T8142 the SEP profile was chosen
only for the boards it had been tried on (J613, J615, J614s, J616s, J614c,
J616c, J714s, K116s, J716c, J713, J715, J813, J815). Every other board with
the same SoC got no profile, and so no SEP and no Touch ID, although each
profile holds only SoC facts: the SEP's shared memory and handshake, its
DART window, and the SPI controller the SoC wires to the sensor.

Select by the SoC compatible, as T8103, T8112, T6000/T6001, T6020/T6021
and T8140 already are. A board without a built-in sensor has nothing on
that bus: the sensor probe finds no part and the SEP runs without
biometrics, as on the M1 and M2 Mac minis.

A device tree that does not describe the SEP at all (the T8142 J704 has
no apple,sep node yet) now gets no profile, so module init keeps saying
the SEP stays disabled instead of failing on the missing node.

The KUnit selection test now checks that the board compatible does not
matter, and the Touch ID list covers the boards this admits.

The profiles were written by Scott Jones (T8122), Michal S. (J614s),
Artyom Astafurov and Jos Dehaes (T6041), Diljit Singh (T6050), Jens Axboe
(K116s), Erik Bourget (J713, J716c) and Nick (T8142).

Fixes: 64ff4f1a3f3ec ("soc: apple: sep: add a T8122 (MacBook Air M3) platform profile")
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
